1
00:00:10,060 --> 00:00:11,780
What are you doing, Lori?

2
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:14,260
I’m looking for my T-shirt.

3
00:00:15,300 --> 00:00:15,900
Your T-shirt?

4
00:00:16,780 --> 00:00:17,700
You’ve got lots!

5
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:20,040
Which one are you looking for?

6
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:22,480
The red one with the flower on it.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,680
I can’t find it anywhere and I’ve got to go out in five minutes.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,420
Where are you going?

9
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:32,140
To the cinema with Lucy and Darcy.

10
00:00:32,540 --> 00:00:33,800
I want to look nice.

11
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:36,960
Oh, I can’t find it!

12
00:00:37,360 --> 00:00:39,741
Do you want to borrow one of mine?

13
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:40,840
Can I?

14
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:44,220
Thanks, Olivia, you’re the best.

15
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:44,880
Sure.

16
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:47,840
Just get one from my room but don’t make a mess.

17
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:49,060
Don’t worry.

18
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:50,920
No mess, I promise.

19
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,840
Can I borrow your red boots, too?

20
00:00:55,440 --> 00:00:57,180
You mean my new ones?

21
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:59,580
No, you can’t.

22
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:00,820
Oh no, really?

23
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:02,060
Why not?

24
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:03,360
They’re really cool!

25
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:14,340
I know I am the world’s best sister, Lori, but there are some times when I have to say ‘no’!

26
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:16,560
Mum?

27
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:19,340
Have we got any plans this weekend?

28
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:21,480
I don’t think so.

29
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:22,260
Why?

30
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:24,600
There’s a party at Nathan’s place.

31
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:25,760
Can I go?

32
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:28,363
Well, that depends …

33
00:01:28,500 --> 00:01:29,120
When is it?

34
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:30,120
Saturday night.

35
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:30,940
8 o’clock.

36
00:01:31,860 --> 00:01:32,320
Sure.

37
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,160
That’s fine.

38
00:01:33,860 --> 00:01:35,200
Where does Nathan live?

39
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:36,680
He’s out in Foxton.

40
00:01:37,361 --> 00:01:39,760
His family have got an amazing house.

41
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:41,060
Foxton?

42
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:44,060
That’s five kilometres from here.

43
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:48,780
And I guess there are no buses at that time of night.

44
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:52,408
Umm, no, I don’t think so.

45
00:01:52,756 --> 00:01:56,040
Well, no actually, there aren’t any buses, no.

46
00:01:56,520 --> 00:01:56,982
Right.

47
00:01:57,700 --> 00:01:58,680
So come on.

48
00:01:59,351 --> 00:02:01,100
Let’s hear the next question, Tom.

49
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:03,260
Any chance of a lift, Mum?

50
00:02:03,660 --> 00:02:07,940
Of course, but I need to pick you up by midnight at the latest.

51
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:09,960
I need my sleep, you know.

52
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:11,529
No problem, Mum.

53
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:12,740
Thanks so much!
